  "slug": "dismutase-history-horizon-3ef8",
  "summary": "New work links Calvin cycle, malic enzyme, isochorismate synthase and FAD synthase perturbations to redox stress, immunity and organ development.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a focus on how perturbing single metabolic enzymes or cofactor pathways reshapes stress physiology and development. In Chlamydomonas, loss or inhibition of PRK in the Calvin cycle triggers light-dependent ROS and autophagy, with ATG8 loss aggravating photosynthetic stress. In Arabidopsis, NAD-malic enzyme subunits ME1 and ME2 show asymmetric interdependence, alter the proteome and metabolism, and affect early fungal penetration. In tomato, knockout of the sole isochorismate synthase lowers pathogen-induced salicylic acid and Pto resistance and impairs phylloquinone-dependent chloroplast function. Arabidopsis cytosolic FAD synthase influences flavin homeostasis, FAD-to-FMN ratios, leaf pigmentation and floral organ development through phytohormone changes. The common thread is metabolic enzymes acting as nodes linking redox, immunity and development.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
